  "weakest_point": "prim_lc_sender is the first life-cycle typed sender gate after prim_lc_sync. It introduces prim_sec_anchor_flop through the AsyncOn=1 sender path, but fixes AsyncOn=1 and ResetValueIsOn=0, so it does not prove the no-flop sender path, ResetValueIsOn=1, all life-cycle encodings, full CDC correctness, security hardening, raw full-state equality, or zero-initialized GPU correctness.",
  "why_this_target": [
    "the sixteenth primitive review accepted prim_lc_sync but kept prim_lc_sender and prim_sec_anchor_flop outside the active claim",
    "prim_lc_sender is the narrowest next life-cycle sender step and directly exercises prim_sec_anchor_flop without introducing the larger req/ack FSM",
    "the upstream module is small and depends on already active lc_ctrl_pkg, prim_flop, and generic flop paths",
    "it is narrower than prim_sync_reqack and prim_diff_decode and avoids clock-gating special behavior",
    "it should reuse the shared Verilator/GPU/runtime flow without target-specific runtime hooks"
  ],
  "rejected_for_now": [
    {
      "target": "prim_sync_reqack",
      "reason": "larger two-clock request/acknowledge FSM than the next life-cycle sender step"
    },
    {
      "target": "prim_diff_decode",
      "reason": "alert/esc-adjacent differential-pair pattern FSM is broader than the next sender gate"
    },
    {
      "target": "prim_clock_gating",
      "reason": "clock-gating latch behavior is clock-special and remains outside the current primitive claim"
    },
    {
      "target": "prim_lc_or_hardened",
